{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"3768 ROUND 3 = BLOCK/5, and one finding proved ME WRONG on a claim I had EMPHATICALLY DEFENDED. Round 4 running. The five collapse into two points.\n\nPOINT 1 (B1/B3/B4) — the batch-step no-op. The reviewer found INDEPENDENTLY exactly what I had found by reading the execution path earlier the same firing, which is good mutual confirmation: a step-level `model:` on a `batch:` step is silently discarded. `interpreter.py:384-395` builds BatchRunRequest from the `batch:` sub-dict only (`model_ladder=tuple(batch.get('model_ladder') or ())`) and never passes the enclosing step's `model:`; `runners.py:128-130` computes `resolve_model_string(ladder[0]) if ladder else None` and comments that an empty ladder means \"no per-step model, NOT a resolved class\". Since `model` IS a legal step key in schema.py's _STEP_ORDER, the declaration VALIDATES and is then thrown away. FIXED: `round_a`/`round_b` now specify `model_ladder: [frontier]` INSIDE `batch:`. A ONE-RUNG ladder keeps the determinism argument used to reject plan-review's three-rung ladder, and resolve_model_string maps the rung through the class table so it follows the RUN's provider. The AC must assert the RESOLVED model at RUNTIME — a YAML-token assertion is exactly what would have hidden this.\n\nPOINT 2 (B2/B5) — **I WAS WRONG, AND THE ERROR IS INSTRUCTIVE.** Two rounds ago I 'defended' a claim the reviewer had bundled with two stale ones, writing that `review_kernel/verify.py:263-269`'s equality test IS operative and 'must not be corrected away'. VERIFIED NOW: `resolve_verifier_model` has ZERO callers under src/rebar — it appears only as its own definition, one docstring mention in model_classes.py:279, and two re-export lines in review_kernel/__init__.py. And `tests/unit/test_code_review_verifier_class.py:171::test_the_superseded_equality_rule_has_no_production_consumers` ENFORCES that nothing calls it, with the docstring instruction 'do not relax the assertion'. **I WROTE THAT TEST MYSELF FOR 172e EARLIER IN THIS SESSION.** So I defended a claim my own earlier test explicitly forbids.\nTHE PRECISE MISTAKE: I checked that the function BODY still contains the equality expression (true) and concluded the RULE IS OPERATIVE (false). **Existence is not reachability.** Verifying a symbol's definition says nothing about whether anything calls it — that needs a caller search, which is exactly what the repo's own tooling guidance (find_referencing_symbols) is for and what I skipped. Retracted in the plan, with the REAL mechanism stated instead: the four undeclared steps inherit the run's single resolved cfg.model because they declare no per-step model at all; resolve_verifier_model is not part of the story, and ADDING a caller would reintroduce the 172e defect.\n\nTHE SWEEP EARNED ITS KEEP AGAIN — THIRD TIME. Retracting the claim left THREE contradictory survivors elsewhere in the plan: a live Problem bullet still asserting 'the verifier downgrade inverts', a sentence saying the four undeclared steps 'still do' depend on the equality rule, and an instruction that the chosen shape 'must also fix' verify.py:269. All three contradicted the retraction; all three fixed. Then a line-by-line probe flagged three MORE, which turned out to be continuation lines of my own retraction blocks (the marker sits on the preceding line) — so I re-verified at the right granularity, checking the five specific dangerous assertions rather than any line containing the word 'equality'. A probe that is too coarse produces false alarms that are as misleading as misses.\n\n3768 history: R1 BLOCK/3, R2 BLOCK/2, R3 BLOCK/5. Not thrash — R1 was staleness from predating eb58, R2 was an unspecified class table, R3 was the no-op plus my wrong defence. Each round found something real."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785601153047840001,"uuid":"576a6756-bc15-4262-bcda-08c1a58738c7"}